# Deep Learning Models for Multi-Object Tracking

## Overview

The DeepStream SDK's multi-object tracker supports multiple deep learning models for enhanced tracking capabilities:

- **Segmentation**: Meta Segment Anything Model 2 (SAM2) for multi-object tracking and segmentation
- **ReID**: NVIDIA TAO ReidentificationNet for re-identification and re-association
- **Pose Estimation**: NVIDIA TAO BodyPose3DNet for 3D tracking and human height estimation

All models utilize TensorRT for accelerated inference. This guide demonstrates how to prepare these models for both dGPU and Jetson platforms.

> **Note**: `<DS_ROOT>` is the path where the [DeepStream repo](https://github.com/NVIDIA/DeepStream) is cloned. The SAM2 conversion tool (`<DS_ROOT>/tools/sam2-onnx-tensorrt`) and the 3D tracking reference apps (`<DS_ROOT>/src/apps/reference_apps`) are **not** packaged in the DeepStream deb/tar packages or the DeepStream Docker image. To use them, clone the DeepStream repo (matching your installed DeepStream release) and use them from the repo:
> ```bash
> git clone https://github.com/NVIDIA/DeepStream.git
> cd DeepStream
> git lfs pull
> ```

## Table of Contents

- [TensorRT Version Compatibility](#tensorrt-version-compatibility)
- [Segmentation](#segmentation)
- [ReID](#reid)
- [Pose Estimation](#pose-estimation)

## TensorRT Version Compatibility

Tracker YAML precision values are `0=FP32`, `1=FP16`, `2=INT8`. They differ
from the DeepStream inference-context enum; do not renumber tracker configs.

TensorRT versions below 11 retain the legacy calibrator, capability-query and
precision-builder API paths. TensorRT 11 uses strongly typed, explicit-batch
networks: FP16 requires a prepared typed/mixed-precision model, and INT8 requires
explicit Q/DQ quantization. A legacy calibration table is not a replacement for
such a model and is rejected, including when loading a cached engine. A precision
setting alone does not convert an FP32 graph, and unsupported requests must not
silently fall back to FP32. The pretrained model commands below do not establish
that those assets have been prepared or qualified for TensorRT 11.

For TensorRT 11, prepare a separately named typed FP16/mixed ONNX offline using
NVIDIA ModelOpt AutoCast (`tools/modelopt/README.md` in the repository), or obtain
a qualified typed export from the model producer. The generic CPU tool accepts
ordinary ONNX operations with representative, correctly preprocessed inputs;
it does not decrypt ETLT, quantize INT8, or supply implementations of custom ops.
No Python conversion or dependency installation runs inside the tracker.

When replacing an ETLT model with its prepared ONNX, change the relevant ReID or
PoseEstimator section together:

```yaml
networkMode: 1
tltEncodedModel: ""  # Remove/clear this; a nonempty ETLT path takes precedence.
onnxFile: "/path/to/model_modelopt_fp16.onnx"
modelEngineFile: "/path/to/model_modelopt_fp16_b100_gpu0_fp16.engine"
```

Keep that model's existing batchSize, inferDims, preprocessing, feature/keypoint
dimensions and FP32 I/O. The engine filename above is illustrative: select a
fresh path appropriate to the actual batch/GPU, not an old ETLT/TRT10 engine.
The ReidentificationNet and BodyPose3DNet download examples below are legacy
ETLT setup instructions, not inputs accepted directly by the offline ONNX tool.
Obtain an authorized matching ONNX export; renaming .etlt to .onnx is not a
conversion. Do not set networkMode to FP32 merely to bypass an FP16 failure.

ReID, pose and segmentation currently exchange FP32 tensors in device memory.
Prepared models must preserve compatible FP32 external I/O and linear layout;
internal computation may use typed FP16 or INT8 Q/DQ. Initialization checks the
engine I/O contract instead of adding automatic per-frame type conversions.
The tracker retains the engine's runtime for ONNX, ETLT and cached-engine paths.

Cached engines must be separately qualified for the selected TensorRT version,
GPU, model, precision and optimization profiles. FP32 engine I/O does not prove
its internal precision, and `networkMode` does not retype an existing engine.
Use a separate cache path when changing the stack/model and rebuild as needed.

Qualify cold builds and cached reloads, batch 1/partial/maximum, invalid-input
errors and repeated lifecycle transitions. Compare ReID embeddings/associations,
pose keypoints and segmentation masks against fixed inputs, and measure warmed-up
latency/throughput/memory against a matched known-good baseline. Compilation and
synthetic numerical checks alone do not establish unchanged tracking accuracy or
performance, or validate another hardware platform.

## Segmentation

The `config_tracker_MaskTracker.yml` configuration enables high-accuracy multi-object tracking and segmentation using SAM2 with memory bank support. This creates a segmentation module based on `config_tracker_module_Segmenter.yml`.

### Model Source

- **Model**: Meta SAM2
- **URL**: [SAM2 GitHub Repository](https://github.com/facebookresearch/sam2)

### Setup Instructions

1. **Prepare Model Files**
   ```bash
   mkdir -p /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream/samples/models/Tracker/
   # Not packaged in deb/tar/Docker; use it from the DeepStream repo clone.
   cd ../../../../../tools/sam2-onnx-tensorrt
   python3 -m venv sam2_env
   source sam2_env/bin/activate
   bash run.sh
   ```

   The following four ONNX files will be generated in the `Tracker` directory:
   - `image_encoder.onnx`
   - `mask_decoder.onnx`
   - `memory_attention.onnx`
   - `memory_encoder.onnx`

   > **TensorRT 11**: all four configured modules request FP16. Each ONNX must
   > already contain qualified FP16/mixed computation while retaining its
   > original external I/O. Supply real multi-input reference tensors for the
   > decoder/memory modules when using AutoCast; random image inputs alone do
   > not cover their state, prompts or masks. Update each module's onnxFile and
   > modelEngineFile to its separately prepared model and fresh engine path.
   > Already typed compatible exports do not need another conversion.
   > TensorRT engines are built at startup from these prepared models; startup
   > does not convert FP32 graphs, download models or install ModelOpt.

2. **Configure DeepStream App**
   - Navigate to `samples/configs/deepstream-app/`
   - Modify the `[tracker]` configuration in your deepstream-app config file:
     ```bash
     ll-config-file=config_tracker_MaskTracker.yml
     ```

3. **Run DeepStream App**
   ```bash
   deepstream-app -c <deepstream-app-config.txt>
   ```

## ReID

The `config_tracker_NvDCF_accuracy.yml` and `config_tracker_NvDeepSORT.yml` configurations support ReID functionality with two options available.

> **Warning**: UFF models have been deprecated by TensorRT. We recommend switching to ONNX or NVIDIA TAO models.

### Option 1: NVIDIA Pretrained TAO ReID Model (Recommended)

#### Model Source

- **Model**: NVIDIA TAO ReidentificationNet
- **URL**: [NVIDIA NGC Catalog](https://catalog.ngc.nvidia.com/orgs/nvidia/teams/tao/models/reidentificationnet)

#### Setup Instructions

1. **Prepare Model Files**
   ```bash
   mkdir -p /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream/samples/models/Tracker/
   wget 'https://api.ngc.nvidia.com/v2/models/nvidia/tao/reidentificationnet/versions/deployable_v1.0/files/resnet50_market1501.etlt' -P /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream/samples/models/Tracker/
   ```

2. **Configure DeepStream App**
   - Navigate to `samples/configs/deepstream-app/`
   - Modify the `[tracker]` configuration in your deepstream-app config file:
     ```bash
     ll-config-file=config_tracker_NvDCF_accuracy.yml
     # or ll-config-file=config_tracker_NvDeepSORT.yml
     ```

3. **Run DeepStream App**
   ```bash
   deepstream-app -c <deepstream-app-config.txt>
   ```

### Option 2: Custom ONNX Model

#### Prerequisites
- Convert your custom model to ONNX format

#### Configuration Parameters
Modify the following parameters in your tracker config file based on your custom model architecture:

```yaml
reidFeatureSize: 128
inferDims: [128, 64, 3]
networkMode: 0
inputOrder: 1
colorFormat: 0
offsets: [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]
netScaleFactor: 1.0000
addFeatureNormalization: 1
onnxFile: "change-to-model-name.onnx"
```

## Pose Estimation

For 3D tracking examples, refer to `../../../../apps/reference_apps/deepstream-tracker-3d` in the DeepStream repo clone (not packaged in deb/tar/Docker). Currently, only NVIDIA TAO BodyPose3DNet is supported.

### Model Source
- **Model**: NVIDIA TAO BodyPose3DNet
- **URL**: [NVIDIA NGC Catalog](https://catalog.ngc.nvidia.com/orgs/nvidia/teams/tao/models/bodypose3dnet)

### Setup Instructions

1. **Prepare Model Files**
   ```bash
   mkdir -p /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream/samples/models/Tracker/
   wget 'https://api.ngc.nvidia.com/v2/models/nvidia/tao/bodypose3dnet/versions/deployable_accuracy_v1.0/files/bodypose3dnet_accuracy.etlt' -P /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream/samples/models/Tracker/
   ```

2. **Configure and Run DeepStream App**

   Visit `../../../../apps/reference_apps` in the DeepStream repo clone (not packaged in deb/tar/Docker) for how to setup and use single and multi-view 3D tracking.

